4b11e797-014e-45f5-97c0-3a0735c9ae9e	ac20995a-cd2b-4183-a53c-4f4ef658a9e5	818c760a-e5d6-42fa-a68d-78bf45f3659d	Ender-3 Max  300x300x340mm	ender-3-max-300x300x340mm	ender-3-max-300x300x340mm	<ul class="a-unordered-list a-vertical a-spacing-mini" data-mce-fragment="1">\n<li data-mce-fragment="1"><span class="a-list-item" data-mce-fragment="1">Meanwell Power Supply: Ender 3 Max comes with UL Certified power supply which with a stable output ensuring the temperature fluctuations are minimized and protecting your printer from unexpected power surges. It can heat the hot bed to 110? in around 10 minutes.</span></li>\n<li data-mce-fragment="1"><span class="a-list-item" data-mce-fragment="1">Tempered Carborundum Glass Platform: Enable the hotbed heat up quicker and prints adhere better without the warping. Ultra smoothness even on the first layer.</span></li>\n<li data-mce-fragment="1"><span class="a-list-item" data-mce-fragment="1">All Metal Extruder, Filament Detector, Dual Cooling Fans in Hot End Kit 2 Sides : promotes better printing result, prevent filament wasting and reduces filament blockage</span></li>\n<li data-mce-fragment="1"><span class="a-list-item" data-mce-fragment="1">Silent Motherboard. Backed by TMC2208 silent driver, reduce noise and wobbling during printing, smoother motion and quieter printing process.; BL touch installation also is available(not included in package, you can buy one if you need. Please flash firmware first if you use a BLTouch).</span></li>\n<li data-mce-fragment="1"><span class="a-list-item" data-mce-fragment="1">Quick Installation: Creality ender 3 max comes almost assembled, which is much easier to assemble than Ender 3. You only need to spend few minutes to attach the gantry with four screws, the screen, filament spool holder, portable handle and plug in some wires.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Product Description</h2>\n<p><img src="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0595/2464/5057/files/ender_1_06ae6aeb-adcf-4773-9984-c001dd77e99c_480x480.jpg?v=1656578886" alt="" data-mce-src="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0595/2464/5057/files/ender_1_06ae6aeb-adcf-4773-9984-c001dd77e99c_480x480.jpg?v=1656578886"></p>\n<h1 class="aplus-h1 aplus-module-1-heading a-text-bold">Creality's Ender 3 Max</h1>\n<p>  </p>\n<div style="text-align: left;"><img style="float: left;" alt="" src="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0595/2464/5057/files/ender_2_72d4b884-a675-45f4-b254-e999c0f282eb_480x480.jpg?v=1656578930" width="337" height="253"></div>\n<h1 class="aplus-h1 aplus-module-1-heading a-text-bold">Creality's Ender 3 Max</h1>\n<p class="aplus-p2 aplus-module-1-description"><span class="a-text-italic">Creality's Ender 3 Max is a basic blast from the past. Here are our first impressions.</span></p>\n<p class="aplus-p2 aplus-module-1-description">The main show of the Creality Ender 3 Max is its hefty slab of a build plate, here in Creality’s impressive-sounding carborundum glass. In normal speak, it’s a 300 x 300 mm flat sheet of glass, with a surface texture that grips the print when hot, and releases it when cold.</p>\n<p class="aplus-p2 aplus-module-1-description">Standing tall above this print area is a Z-axis of 340 mm, 60 mm less than on the Creality CR-10, the printer the Ender 3 Max is closest.</p>\n<p class="aplus-p2 aplus-module-1-description"> </p>\n<div style="text-align: right;"><img style="float: right;" alt="" src="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0595/2464/5057/files/ender_3_813ec7c8-df4c-4e49-96d4-ce1a582dd506_480x480.jpg?v=1656578983" width="287" height="215"></div>\n<h1 class="aplus-h1 aplus-module-1-heading a-text-bold">Creality's Ender 3 Max</h1>\n<p class="aplus-p2 aplus-module-1-description">The print head itself has a curious design, with the push-fit coupling for the Bowden tube recessed below the top of the print head housing. If this remains as is on the finalized Ender 3 Max, users might have a tedious time removing the print head housing before accessing the push-fit for the Bowden tube. Maintenance and blockage-clearing action will be a pain in the posterior.</p>\n<p class="aplus-p2 aplus-module-1-description"> </p>\n<div style="text-align: left;"><img style="float: left;" alt="" src="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0595/2464/5057/files/ender_4_3e5bc043-11b6-4336-aadc-7d2cb83a3bf3_480x480.jpg?v=1656579015" width="307" height="230"></div>\n<h1 class="aplus-h1 aplus-module-1-heading a-text-bold">Creality's Ender 3 Max</h1>\n<p class="aplus-p2 aplus-module-1-description">Creality indicated to us that the Ender 3 Max will ship with a “silent” mainboard. We assumed that the printer would ship with some flavor of TMC stepper motor drivers for quiet operation. This doesn’t seem to be the case, though, with this preview printer whirring and whining away just as the Ender 3 did back in 2018. Cracking the printer open, we find Creality’s new 4.2.2 32-bit board, rocking non-replaceable A4988 stepper motor drivers</p>\n<p class="aplus-p2 aplus-module-1-description"> </p>\n<div style="text-align: right;"><img style="float: right;" src="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0595/2464/5057/files/ender_5_4a9657d0-ba74-4684-a282-f4923d6d7eff_480x480.jpg?v=1656579061" width="295" height="221"></div>\n<h1 class="aplus-h1 aplus-module-1-heading a-text-bold">Creality's Ender 3 Max</h1>\n<p class="aplus-p2 aplus-module-1-description">Small change aside, we do quite like the Creality Ender 3 Max. It’s shaping up to be a decent simple big-volume printer, though there’s plenty of room for improvement both on Creality’s part pre-launch and for the user customizing their machine</p>\n<p class="aplus-p2 aplus-module-1-description">We had initially pondered why the company would bother with a machine that, held against its more recent efforts, is a tad basic. When you use those pricier machines week in week out it gets all too easy to lose sight of how simplicity can be a good thing</p>\n<p class="aplus-p2 aplus-module-1-description"> </p>\n<p class="aplus-p2 aplus-module-1-description"> </p>\n<p class="aplus-p2 aplus-module-1-description"> </p>\n<p class="aplus-p2 aplus-module-1-description"> </p>	<ul class="a-unordered-list a-vertical a-spacing-mini" data-mce-fragment="1">\n<li data-mce-fragment="1"><span class="a-list-item" data-mce-fragment="1">Meanwell Power Supply: Ender 3 Max comes with UL Certified power supply which with a stable output ensuring the temperature fluctuations are minimized and protecting your printer from unexpected power surges. It can heat the hot bed to 110? in around 10 minutes.</span></li>\n<li data-mce-fragment="1"><span class="a-list-item" data-mce-fragment="1">Tempered Carborundum Glass Platform: Enable the hotbed heat up quicker and prints adhere better without the warping. Ultra smoothness even on the first layer.</span></li>\n<li data-mce-fragment="1"><span class="a-list-item" data-mce-fragment="1">All Metal Extruder, Filament Detector, Dual Cooling Fans in Hot End Kit 2 Sides : promotes better printing result, prevent filament wasting and reduces filament blockage</span></li>\n<li data-mce-fragment="1"><span class="a-list-item" data-mce-fragment="1">Silent Motherboard. Backed by TMC2208 silent driver, reduce noise and wobbling during printing, smoother motion and quieter printing process.; BL touch installation also is available(not included in package, you can buy one if you need. Please flash firmware first if you use a BLTouch).</span></li>\n<li data-mce-fragment="1"><span class="a-list-item" data-mce-fragment="1">Quick Installation: Creality ender 3 max comes almost assembled, which is much easier to assemble than Ender 3. You only need to spend few minutes to attach the gantry with four screws, the screen, filament spool holder, portable handle and plug in some wires.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Product Description</h2>\n<p><img src="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0595/2464/5057/files/ender_1_06ae6aeb-adcf-4773-9984-c001dd77e99c_480x480.jpg?v=1656578886" alt="" data-mce-src="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0595/2464/5057/files/ender_1_06ae6aeb-adcf-4773-9984-c001dd77e99c_480x480.jpg?v=1656578886"></p>\n<h1 class="aplus-h1 aplus-module-1-heading a-text-bold">Creality's Ender 3 Max</h1>\n<p>  </p>\n<div style="text-align: left;"><img style="float: left;" alt="" src="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0595/2464/5057/files/ender_2_72d4b884-a675-45f4-b254-e999c0f282eb_480x480.jpg?v=1656578930" width="337" height="253"></div>\n<h1 class="aplus-h1 aplus-module-1-heading a-text-bold">Creality's Ender 3 Max</h1>\n<p class="aplus-p2 aplus-module-1-description"><span class="a-text-italic">Creality's Ender 3 Max is a basic blast from the past. Here are our first impressions.</span></p>\n<p class="aplus-p2 aplus-module-1-description">The main show of the Creality Ender 3 Max is its hefty slab of a build plate, here in Creality’s impressive-sounding carborundum glass. In normal speak, it’s a 300 x 300 mm flat sheet of glass, with a surface texture that grips the print when hot, and releases it when cold.</p>\n<p class="aplus-p2 aplus-module-1-description">Standing tall above this print area is a Z-axis of 340 mm, 60 mm less than on the Creality CR-10, the printer the Ender 3 Max is closest.</p>\n<p class="aplus-p2 aplus-module-1-description"> </p>\n<div style="text-align: right;"><img style="float: right;" alt="" src="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0595/2464/5057/files/ender_3_813ec7c8-df4c-4e49-96d4-ce1a582dd506_480x480.jpg?v=1656578983" width="287" height="215"></div>\n<h1 class="aplus-h1 aplus-module-1-heading a-text-bold">Creality's Ender 3 Max</h1>\n<p class="aplus-p2 aplus-module-1-description">The print head itself has a curious design, with the push-fit coupling for the Bowden tube recessed below the top of the print head housing. If this remains as is on the finalized Ender 3 Max, users might have a tedious time removing the print head housing before accessing the push-fit for the Bowden tube. Maintenance and blockage-clearing action will be a pain in the posterior.</p>\n<p class="aplus-p2 aplus-module-1-description"> </p>\n<div style="text-align: left;"><img style="float: left;" alt="" src="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0595/2464/5057/files/ender_4_3e5bc043-11b6-4336-aadc-7d2cb83a3bf3_480x480.jpg?v=1656579015" width="307" height="230"></div>\n<h1 class="aplus-h1 aplus-module-1-heading a-text-bold">Creality's Ender 3 Max</h1>\n<p class="aplus-p2 aplus-module-1-description">Creality indicated to us that the Ender 3 Max will ship with a “silent” mainboard. We assumed that the printer would ship with some flavor of TMC stepper motor drivers for quiet operation. This doesn’t seem to be the case, though, with this preview printer whirring and whining away just as the Ender 3 did back in 2018. Cracking the printer open, we find Creality’s new 4.2.2 32-bit board, rocking non-replaceable A4988 stepper motor drivers</p>\n<p class="aplus-p2 aplus-module-1-description"> </p>\n<div style="text-align: right;"><img style="float: right;" src="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0595/2464/5057/files/ender_5_4a9657d0-ba74-4684-a282-f4923d6d7eff_480x480.jpg?v=1656579061" width="295" height="221"></div>\n<h1 class="aplus-h1 aplus-module-1-heading a-text-bold">Creality's Ender 3 Max</h1>\n<p class="aplus-p2 aplus-module-1-description">Small change aside, we do quite like the Creality Ender 3 Max. It’s shaping up to be a decent simple big-volume printer, though there’s plenty of room for improvement both on Creality’s part pre-launch and for the user customizing their machine</p>\n<p class="aplus-p2 aplus-module-1-description">We had initially pondered why the company would bother with a machine that, held against its more recent efforts, is a tad basic. When you use those pricier machines week in week out it gets all too easy to lose sight of how simplicity can be a good thing</p>\n<p class="aplus-p2 aplus-module-1-description"> </p>\n<p class="aplus-p2 aplus-module-1-description"> </p>\n<p class="aplus-p2 aplus-module-1-description"> </p>\n<